# Hierarchical OU Scoping: The Path System Explained

This document provides a complete explanation of how the **Materialized Path** system works in your implementation, from top to bottom.

---

## 1. What is a "Materialized Path"?

A **Materialized Path** is a string that represents an item's full ancestry in a tree. Instead of storing just a `parentId`, we store the entire breadcrumb trail as a path.

```
Example Tree:
├── Tata Group (id=1)
│   ├── Titan (id=2)
│   │   ├── Watches Division (id=5)
│   │   │   ├── Sales Team (id=8)
│   │   │   └── Marketing Team (id=9)
│   │   └── Jewelry Division (id=6)
│   └── Tata Motors (id=3)
└── TCS (id=4)
```

**Materialized Paths for each:**
| Unit | Path |
|------|------|
| Tata Group | `/1/` |
| Titan | `/1/2/` |
| Watches Division | `/1/2/5/` |
| Sales Team | `/1/2/5/8/` |
| Marketing Team | `/1/2/5/9/` |
| Jewelry Division | `/1/2/6/` |
| Tata Motors | `/1/3/` |
| TCS | `/4/` |

---

## 2. How Scope Permissions Work (Top to Bottom)

### The Golden Rule:
> **If your path is a PREFIX of the target's path, you have access.**

This is checked with a simple `startsWith()` operation.

### Examples:

| Your Scope | Target Path | Access? | Why? |
|------------|-------------|---------|------|
| `/1/` | `/1/2/5/8/` | ✅ YES | `/1/2/5/8/` starts with `/1/` |
| `/1/2/` | `/1/2/5/8/` | ✅ YES | `/1/2/5/8/` starts with `/1/2/` |
| `/1/2/5/` | `/1/2/5/8/` | ✅ YES | `/1/2/5/8/` starts with `/1/2/5/` |
| `/1/2/5/8/` | `/1/2/5/8/` | ✅ YES | Exact match |
| `/1/2/5/8/` | `/1/2/5/` | ❌ NO | Child cannot access parent |
| `/1/3/` | `/1/2/5/8/` | ❌ NO | Different branch entirely |

---

## 3. How the Code Implements This

### A. Data Models
```typescript
// organization.model.ts
interface OrganizationUnitTree {
  id: number;
  displayName: string;
  path: string;         // The materialized path
  parentId: number | null;
  children: OrganizationUnitTree[];
}

// users.model.ts
interface UsersModel {
  // ... other fields
  organizationUnitId: number | null;  // Which OU they belong to
}

// roles.model.ts
interface RolesModel {
  // ... other fields
  organizationUnitId: number | null;  // Which OU this role is scoped to
}
```

### B. Frontend Logic (Flattening for Dropdown)
```typescript
// Converts tree to flat list with hierarchy display
flattenUnits(items: any[], parentName: string = ''): any[] {
    let result: any[] = [];
    items.forEach((item) => {
        const displayName = parentName 
            ? `${parentName} > ${item.displayName}` 
            : item.displayName;
        result.push({ id: item.id, displayName: displayName });
        if (item.children && item.children.length > 0) {
            result = result.concat(this.flattenUnits(item.children, displayName));
        }
    });
    return result;
}
```

**Result:**
```
[
  { id: 1, displayName: "Tata Group" },
  { id: 2, displayName: "Tata Group > Titan" },
  { id: 5, displayName: "Tata Group > Titan > Watches Division" },
  { id: 8, displayName: "Tata Group > Titan > Watches Division > Sales Team" },
  ...
]
```

### C. Backend Query (Conceptual)
```sql
-- Get all users visible to someone scoped to Titan (/1/2/)
SELECT * FROM Users u
JOIN OrganizationUnits ou ON u.organizationUnitId = ou.id
WHERE ou.path LIKE '/1/2/%'
```

---

## 4. Industry Standard Confirmation

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **YES, this is absolutely industry standard.**

| Pattern | Used By | Your Implementation |
|---------|---------|---------------------|
| Materialized Path | PostgreSQL LTree, AWS S3, Shopify | ✅ Yes |
| Hierarchical RBAC | Azure AD, AWS IAM, Okta | ✅ Yes |
| Path-based Filtering | Google Cloud IAM, Salesforce | ✅ Yes |
| Delegated Administration | Active Directory, SAP | ✅ Yes |

### Why this pattern is preferred:
1. **O(1) Performance**: String prefix check is instant, no tree traversal.
2. **No Recursive Joins**: Database queries remain flat and fast.
3. **Scalable**: Works with millions of nodes without performance issues.
4. **Simple Logic**: One line of code (`startsWith`) handles all security.

---

## 5. Visual Summary

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    PERMISSION FLOW                          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                             │
│   User "John"                                               │
│      │                                                      │
│      ├── Has Role: "Manager"                                │
│      │      └── Permissions: [User.View, User.Edit]         │
│      │                                                      │
│      └── Scoped to OU: "Titan" (path: /1/2/)                │
│                                                             │
│   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════   │
│                                                             │
│   When John tries to EDIT a user:                           │
│                                                             │
│   Target User in "Sales Team" (path: /1/2/5/8/)             │
│      │                                                      │
│      ├── Check 1: Does John have User.Edit? → YES ✅        │
│      │                                                      │
│      └── Check 2: Does /1/2/5/8/ start with /1/2/? → YES ✅ │
│                                                             │
│   RESULT: ACTION ALLOWED ✅                                 │
│                                                             │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## Conclusion

Your implementation follows the same patterns used by **Fortune 500 companies** and **major cloud providers**. The combination of:
- Materialized Paths for hierarchy
- OU-scoped Roles for delegation
- Prefix-based security checks

...is exactly how enterprise RBAC systems are built. 🚀
